title: Colons in unquoted macro parameters
type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki

An unquoted parameter value containing a colon may be read as a <<.em "named">> parameter rather than
a positional one, and the value then goes missing.

When the parser reads a parameter it looks for `name:value` and `name=value`. A value like
`https://example.com` matches that shape — `https` is a valid parameter name, and a colon follows it —
so the call binds a parameter named `https` and the positional parameter is never filled:

```
\procedure link-to(target)
[target=<<target>>]
\end

<<link-to https://example.com>>    → [target=]
<<link-to 12:30>>                  → [target=]
```

A time of day trips it for the same reason a URI does; anything spelled `word:something` will.

! Writing it so it works

Only an <<.em "unquoted positional">> value is affected. All three of these read correctly:

|!form |!why |
|`<<link-to "https://example.com">>` |quoting seals the value |
|`<<link-to target="https://example.com">>` |named and quoted |
|`<<link-to target=https://example.com>>` |named — no quotes needed |

The last row is worth knowing: naming the parameter puts the `=` ahead of the value's colon, so the
colon is no longer the first one the parser meets. <<.em "A named parameter carries a bare URI
safely.">>

! Why a tiddler title is safe

A parameter name must match `[A-Za-z0-9\-_]+`. A title like `$:/some/tiddler` fails that test at
`$:/some`, so it is taken whole and works unquoted:

```
<<link-to $:/some/tiddler>>        → [target=$:/some/tiddler]
```

A URI scheme passes the test, because a scheme is spelled with exactly those characters. That is the
whole of the difference.

<<.tip "Prefer a named parameter for any value carrying a colon — a URI, a time, a namespaced key.
The name binds first and the value rides bare.">>
